# Information architecture

## Core user flow

1. **Mood**: capture how the user feels (quick pick, text, or voice later)
2. **Match**: show a short ranked set of tastes/places/recipes
3. **Act**: save, cook, or go (directions / details)
4. **Remember**: optional history and preferences that improve future matches

Keep this sequence intact. Do not bury mood capture behind long onboarding.

## Site / app map (v0)

| Area | Purpose | Notes |
|------|---------|--------|
| Home / landing | Brand + one CTA into mood flow | `src/app/page.tsx` |
| PRD | Public product requirements | `src/app/prd/page.tsx` → `/prd` |
| Strategy | GTM, growth & monetization | `src/app/strategy/page.tsx` → `/strategy` |
| Brand Guide | Brand voice & visual system | `src/app/brand/page.tsx` → `/brand` |
| Mood entry | Select or describe mood | Fast; one job |
| Results | Short list with “why it fits” | No endless scroll walls |
| Detail | Single pick: recipe, place, or tasting note | One primary action |
| About / privacy | Trust and product stance | Linked from footer |

## Content hierarchy

- One job per screen/section; one headline; one short support line
- Brand name must remain a primary signal on marketing surfaces
- Prefer short ranked picks over filter-heavy catalogs
- Secondary content (stats, schedules, promos) stays out of the first viewport
- Sections are separated by space and tone, not dividers (`design-system.mdc`)

## Navigation rules

- Primary chrome: Taste · Stats · Profile tab bar (no site header or footer)
- Doc pages: brand home link in the hero only
- Avoid mega-menus, pill clusters, and competing CTAs on the same surface

## Naming

- User-facing: mood, taste, match, pick
- Avoid: “algorithm,” “optimization,” “wellness score” in UI copy

## Punctuation in copy

- **No em dashes** anywhere: UI copy, metadata, docs, rules, or code comments.
  Use a period, comma, or colon; parentheses for a true aside
- En dashes are fine for numeric ranges only (18-35, 50-150)
- One idea per sentence beats one sentence with a hinge in the middle
